# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so it must
# never contain a real value. On Vercel these go in Project Settings ->
# Environment Variables, not in a file.
#
# Nothing here is required for `npm run dev` to serve the marketing pages. The
# contact form and checkout are the two things that need real values, and each
# fails honestly (503 "not configured") rather than silently when they are absent.

# =============================================================================
# Stripe
# =============================================================================
#
# Start in TEST mode. Every value below has a `sk_test_` / test-mode twin and
# the integration is identical, so there is no reason to touch live keys until
# a real card has been through the flow with 4242 4242 4242 4242.

# Secret key. Server-side only — it is never sent to the browser, and anything
# that would put it in a client bundle is a bug. Rotate immediately if leaked.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...

# The price id (`price_...`, NOT `prod_...`) of the one-time price.
# Create it in the Stripe dashboard as a one-time price.
#
# Taken from the environment so a client cannot name its own price and buy the
# product for a penny. There is a test for this.
STRIPE_PRICE_ONE_TIME=price_...

# The price id of the recurring monthly price, for the subscribe button.
#
# Leave it unset until the subscription is ready: the button is gated on
# PRO_AVAILABLE in src/lib/pricing.ts and does not render while that is false.
# The flag and this variable have to move together, or the button appears and
# reports not_configured.
STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY=price_...

# Origin used to build Stripe's return URL. Set explicitly rather than read from
# the request, because the request's Host header is attacker-supplied on some
# hosts and it ends up in a redirect.
#
# Locally: http://localhost:3000 under `vercel dev` (NOT Astro's 4321).
#
# On Vercel: set this in the PRODUCTION scope only. Do not create it for Preview
# — preview URLs change on every push, so any fixed value is wrong by the next
# deploy. The adapter derives the origin from VERCEL_URL on preview deployments
# instead. Vercel will not save an empty value, and it should not: the variable
# is meant to be absent there, not blank.
SITE_ORIGIN=https://example.com

# Merchant of record. LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you have a reason not to.
#
# Defaults to on. Only the exact string 'false' disables it, so a typo or a
# forgotten variable leaves Stripe carrying the VAT/sales-tax liability rather
# than you — that asymmetry is deliberate and there is a test for it.
#
# Set it to false ONLY if your sandbox rejects the session with a
# `managed_payments_unavailable` error, which means the account is not approved
# for Managed Payments yet. Unset it again the day approval lands, and never set
# it in production without deciding, on purpose, to owe the tax yourself.
# STRIPE_MANAGED_PAYMENTS=false

# Signing secret for licence keys. Any long random string:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Keys are DERIVED from the Stripe customer id and this secret, never stored, so
# the same customer always gets the same key and a lost key can be re-issued
# with nothing on our side. The flip side: rotating this invalidates every key
# ever issued, and there is no way to re-sign them. Treat it as permanent, and
# use a different value in test and live.
LICENSE_SIGNING_SECRET=

# From the Stripe webhook endpoint you create for checkout.session.completed.
# Without it the endpoint refuses every delivery -- which is correct: an
# unverified webhook would let anyone POST a fake event and be emailed a working
# licence key.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# =============================================================================
# Resend
# =============================================================================

# Resend API key, shared by the licence email and the contact form.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...

# Envelope sender for the licence email. Must be on a domain verified in Resend,
# with its SPF and DKIM records added to DNS. Without those the mail is accepted
# and then filed as spam.
#
# If this is unset the webhook still succeeds and simply sends nothing, so the
# site works before email is set up — the key is already on screen at checkout,
# so the email is a backup delivery path, not the primary one.
LICENSE_FROM_EMAIL=Example <keys@example.com>

# --- Contact form (/contact -> api/contact.ts) --------------------------------
#
# All three below are required for the form to accept a message: without them
# the endpoint answers 503 and the page says it is not accepting messages, which
# is the only honest failure mode for a form -- accepting a message and dropping
# it is worse.

# Where contact-form mail is delivered. A plain address, e.g. you@gmail.com.
#
# There is deliberately NO default in the source. This address is never sent to
# the browser -- not in the HTML, not in a JSON response -- which is the whole
# point: the page posts to an endpoint and is never told where the message goes.
# A default in `src/server/contact.ts` would put a real inbox into git and undo
# that for the sake of one environment variable.
CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Envelope sender. Must be on a domain verified in Resend, with SPF and DKIM in
# DNS, or the mail is accepted and then filed as spam. The sender's own address
# goes in Reply-To, so hitting Reply in the inbox answers the customer.
CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=Example <contact@example.com>

# HMAC secret for the form's anti-spam token:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 48
#
# The token is a signed timestamp, issued when someone starts typing and checked
# on submit -- it is what stops a bot POSTing straight at the endpoint, and what
# measures whether the form was filled in faster than a human could type. Use a
# different value from LICENSE_SIGNING_SECRET; rotating this one costs at most a
# "please try again" for anyone mid-message at the moment of deploy.
CONTACT_SIGNING_SECRET=

# --- Email list (the sign-up box -> api/subscribe.ts) -------------------------
#
# All three of the below are required for the sign-up box to accept an address:
# without them the endpoint answers 503 and the box says the list is not
# switched on yet. Same reasoning as the contact form -- a box that takes an
# address and drops it is worse than one that admits it is not working, and
# worse still than the form, because there is no reply anyone is waiting for
# and so nobody ever finds out.
#
# The signing secret is CONTACT_SIGNING_SECRET above, shared on purpose: a
# token from either form proves the same fact, so a second secret would be a
# second thing to rotate and nothing else. RESEND_API_KEY is also shared, and
# needs the contacts scope as well as the send scope.

# The Resend segment the address is filed under. Create it in the Resend
# dashboard (Contacts -> Segments) and paste its id here; it looks like a UUID.
# Segments are what Resend's audiences became when contacts went global to the
# account: a contact created without one is still a contact, so this is the
# only thing separating sign-ups from this box from every other address the
# account holds.
#
# To find the id without leaving the terminal:
#
#     curl -s https://api.resend.com/segments \
#       -H "Authorization: Bearer $RESEND_API_KEY"
#
# There is deliberately NO default in the source. A dead id would mean sign-ups
# failing in production with nobody watching, and a real one would be somebody
# else's mailing list committed to git.
RESEND_SEGMENT_ID=

# The name of the Resend custom event fired once the address is on the list.
# It is what the welcome automation triggers on: in the Resend dashboard,
# Automations -> trigger step -> Custom Event, and the name typed there has to
# match this string character for character.
#
#     curl -X POST https://api.resend.com/events/send \
#       -H "Authorization: Bearer $RESEND_API_KEY" \
#       -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#       -d '{"event":"mailing-list-signup","email":"you@example.com"}'
#
# Required, and with no default in the source, because a wrong name fails
# silently in the worst way there is: Resend accepts the event, every sign-up
# reports success, the address really does land on the list, and the welcome
# email is never sent to anybody. The endpoint refuses sign-ups until this is
# set rather than run with half of the arrangement wired up.
RESEND_SIGNUP_EVENT=mailing-list-signup

# =============================================================================
# Spot metal prices (-> api/spot.ts)
# =============================================================================
#
# /api/spot serves a cached snapshot and refreshes it from metals.dev when the
# last refresh was more than twenty hours ago. There is no cron: the reader who
# happens to arrive after the interval pays for the refresh and everybody after
# them is served from the cache.
#
# The feed's free tier is a hundred calls a MONTH, so two guards in
# src/server/spot.ts decide whether a call is spent: the time of the last fetch,
# and a month-to-date call count. Both live in the cached document, because a
# function instance is not a place to keep state.
#
# Setting it up, in this order:
#
#   1. Vercel dashboard -> Storage -> Blob -> create a store, connect it to this
#      project. That injects BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN; never paste it in by hand.
#   2. Set METALS_DEV_API_KEY below in the Production scope.
#   3. Deploy, then request /api/spot once. There is no document yet, so the
#      handler calls the feed, writes spot.json and answers with the new figures.
#   4. Copy that object's public URL out of the dashboard into SPOT_CACHE_URL.
#      It is stable across writes, so this is a one-time step.
#
# Do not stop after 3: a document whose URL the handler has not been given is a
# document whose call count it cannot read, and it would refresh on every
# invocation.

# Absolute https URL of the cached snapshot -- the blob from step 4.
#
# Unset is a working state: every page still shows the built-in reading from
# src/data/spot-snapshot.ts, labelled with the time that reading was taken.
#
# The endpoint refuses a URL that is not https, that points at a price feed, or
# that carries an api_key/token/secret query parameter. A key in a URL is a key
# in this platform's logs, the CDN's logs and the next request's Referer.
SPOT_CACHE_URL=

# metals.dev API key.
#
# Production scope. PREVIEW DEPLOYMENTS SHOULD NOT HAVE IT: without it the
# endpoint reads production's cached document and refreshes nothing, which is
# exactly what a preview should do rather than spending the month's allowance on
# a branch.
#
# The same key is what `npm run spot` uses to refresh the built-in fallback in
# src/data/spot-snapshot.ts -- the reading the static build bakes into every
# page, for crawlers and for readers with no JavaScript. That is a separate
# thing from the cache and has no schedule to keep.
METALS_DEV_API_KEY=

# Optional. A Vercel Deploy Hook URL (Project Settings -> Git -> Deploy Hooks).
#
# What it fixes: readers with JavaScript get a current price from /api/spot, but
# a crawler and a reader with JavaScript off keep whatever the last BUILD
# rendered into the HTML. That figure only changes when the site is rebuilt, so
# without this it is as old as your last deploy -- and a six-month-old silver
# price in a Google result discredits the page it is on.
#
# With it set, a successful daily refresh pokes the hook, the site rebuilds, and
# `npm run build` bakes in the price that refresh just fetched (it reads the
# cached document, which costs no metals.dev calls -- see
# scripts/prebuild-spot.mjs). About one deploy a day; it cannot loop, because a
# build never calls the feed and a deploy never calls /api/spot.
#
# TREAT IT AS A SECRET. Anyone holding this URL can trigger deploys. It is never
# written to a log, not even when poking it fails.
SPOT_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL=

# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is NOT listed here on purpose. Connecting a Blob store
# to the project injects it into every scope automatically; a copy of it in a
# file is a copy to rotate. Without it the endpoint reads the cache and never
# writes, and says so in the log.

# =============================================================================
# Numista (build-time only)
# =============================================================================
#
# A catalogue API used while researching coins, by the import scripts under
# scripts/ and by nothing else. NOT needed to build or serve the site: the
# catalogue ships as committed TypeScript (src/data/coin-generated.ts, written
# from data/coins/*.tsv), so a visitor's request never touches Numista and
# neither does `astro build`.
#
# It therefore never reaches the browser, no numista.com origin belongs in the
# CSP of vercel.json / netlify.toml / public/_headers, and /privacy needs no new
# line for it. Set it locally; there is no reason for it to exist in the Vercel
# dashboard at all.
#
# The allowance is 2,000 calls a month, which is generous for a person looking
# coins up one series at a time and would be gone in an afternoon of scripted
# enumeration. Anything that walks the catalogue caches its responses under
# data/ so re-running an import costs nothing.
NUMISTA_CLIENT_ID=
NUMISTA_API_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# PCGS CoinFacts (build-time research credential, never at runtime)
# =============================================================================
#
# The grade prices. Same rules as Numista: read by `npm run pcgs` under
# scripts/ and by nothing else, nothing under src/ may read it, no page may call
# this API, no api.pcgs.com origin belongs in the CSP, and /privacy needs no new
# line for it.
#
# THE ALLOWANCE IS ONE HUNDRED CALLS A DAY PER KEY, and the endpoint answers for
# one (PCGS number, grade) pair -- so a hundred calls is about five coins and a
# series is weeks rather than an afternoon. `npm run pcgs` is therefore a ledger
# with a fetcher attached: every answer is committed to data/pcgs/<series>.json,
# a pair is never asked twice, and each key's daily count is kept in that file
# rather than in the process. Extra keys are rotated and counted separately; a
# key is identified in the ledger by eight characters of a digest, never by its
# value.
COINFACTS_API_KEY=
COINFACTS_API_KEY_2=
COINFACTS_API_KEY_3=
